    "explanation": "Article 52 is a simple but foundational provision that establishes the office of the President of India as the constitutional head of the Union executive. Despite its brevity, this article creates the highest constitutional office in the Indian republic — the President is the head of state, the supreme commander of the armed forces, and the formal repository of all executive power of the Union.\n\nThe President's role in India's parliamentary system is analogous to the British monarch — a constitutional head who acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers headed by the Prime Minister. However, unlike a hereditary monarch, the President is elected for a fixed term through an indirect election process specified in Article 54.\n\nThe President's powers, though exercised on ministerial advice (Article 74), are extensive: appointing the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers (Article 75), summoning and proroguing Parliament (Article 85), assenting to legislation (Article 111), issuing ordinances (Article 123), declaring emergencies (Articles 352, 356, 360), and exercising the power of pardon (Article 72).",
